  "headline": "Builders' weaker summer quarter stands, LGI has narrowed its closings guidance, mortgage rates sit near 7.3%, costs stay high and KB's land chief retires.",
  "sections": {
    "national": [
      "Nine builders have now reported the summer quarter: Lennar and KB Home for quarters ended August 31, and D.R. Horton, PulteGroup, M/I Homes, Meritage, Century Communities, NVR and LGI Homes for September 30. All nine describe it as weaker than the quarter before. Net orders fell everywhere they are reported (Horton down 7%, Lennar down 9%, Pulte down 6%, KB down 12%, M/I down 2%, Meritage down 15%, Century down 4%, NVR down 7%), and LGI's are down 30% for the year to date. On Nov 3, LGI called its quarter strong, with 1,222 closings up 10.4%, yet narrowed 2026 closings guidance to 4,600 to 4,800 homes on slower sales as affordability weighed [8].",
      "Margins are still compressed year over year: Horton's home sales margin was 20.2% for fiscal 2026 against 21.5%, Lennar 15.8% against 17.5%, Pulte 24.8% against 26.2%. Rates are the stated cause. Lennar puts the 30-year mortgage at about 7% and the 10-year Treasury near 5%, up from 6.4% to 6.5% earlier in the year, and ties the move to energy-driven inflation from tension with Iran. The 30-year fixed rate was 7.28% on Nov 19 and the 10-year 5.04%. HousingWire says the Fed is staying hawkish even with lower energy prices [60], and that sub-7% mortgages need a 10-year yield below 4.85% and 2% mortgage spreads [59].",
      "Cancellations are where the third quarter breaks from the second. Horton's rose to 23% in its September quarter from 20% a year earlier, and 19% for the fiscal year against 18%. NVR's rate rose to 21.0% from 19.4%, and LGI's reached 48.9% for the first nine months against 28.1% a year earlier, which LGI attributes to more buyers being unable to secure or keep mortgage financing. Builder shares reflect it unevenly. Through Nov 23, Lennar is down 22.0% year to date, NVR 24.6% and KB 18.8%, while Pulte is up 6.1% and Toll 7.3% [1].",
      "Horton ended September with 17,700 unsold homes, 8,800 of them finished, expects incentives to stay elevated through fiscal 2027, and guides 79,000 to 84,000 closings for that year against 84,202 in fiscal 2026. Construction materials PPI was up 12.1% year over year in October and lumber 6.4%. On Nov 13, NAHB said building material price inflation remains high despite lower energy prices [53]. On Nov 9 it said wage growth rebounded while real wages kept falling [54]. On Nov 23, hot-rolled steel futures were up 49.0% year to date, diesel 74.8% and WTI crude 40.7% [3]."
    ],
    "west": [
      "The third-quarter filings do speak to California, through each builder's western segment, and the read splits by builder. Horton's Southwest region, which includes California, Arizona, Nevada, New Mexico and Hawaii, lost the most ground on buyer commitment. Its fiscal 2026 cancellation rate rose to 19% from 15%, the largest increase of Horton's six regions, and closings fell 5% to 9,013, which Horton attributes particularly to Las Vegas and Southern California. Lennar's West segment, which includes California, is its weakest. Gross margin was 12.1% against 16.3% a year earlier, the lowest of its four segments, and the filing cites lower average prices in California among other states from base price cuts and more incentives.",
      "Pulte's West segment, which also includes California, had closings down 18%, orders down 15% and a 21% cancellation rate, the highest of its six segments, and pre-tax income fell 57%. KB's West Coast segment (California, Idaho and Washington) is the exception. Net orders rose 8% to 937, backlog value rose 22% to $1.02 billion, average community count rose 25% to 111, and the cancellation rate improved to 16% from 18%. Prices are giving: average selling price fell 6% to $641,800 and segment gross margin slipped to 16.7%. KB's real estate chief, Albert Praw, retires Nov 30 and will advise on land relationships and transactions for up to a year [5].",
      "Century's West segment is California and Washington, and it is where the first real write-down landed. It lost $13.5 million before tax in the quarter, against a $21.2 million profit a year earlier, after $19.1 million of impairment charges. Sumitomo Forestry closed its Tri Pointe purchase on May 14, so quarterly segment disclosure has ended. On Nov 2, Wafra and Hunt Companies launched Strata Land Partners as builders face a land-capital squeeze [50][51]. On Nov 6, a Lennar spinoff paid $42 million for a regional land portfolio [48]. Land banker shares are down this year: Millrose 16.7%, Forestar 8.0%, Five Point 19.7% [2]."
    ],
    "ventura": [
      "No public builder discloses at county level. Ventura appears in none of these filings. Anything county-specific attributed to a builder filing is extrapolation. KB Home is actively selling at Beltramo Ranch in the county, and the new-home pipeline countywide is thin for a county of this size. City Ventures, a private builder, held a grand opening for Walnut Park in Simi Valley on Nov 14 [42].",
      "The resale side, from Redfin's October county data in the resale panel on this board: median sale price $862,100, up 0.5% from a year earlier, on 509 sales, down 12.7%, with a 52-day median time on market and sales at 98.2% of list. Next door, the Los Angeles Case-Shiller index was up 1.4% year over year in August.",
      "The two items that belong on the radar are regulatory, not corporate. The 2025 California Building Standards Code and local County amendments took effect for all applications submitted on or after January 1, 2026, and AB 1308 now requires the County to inspect completed residential work of one to ten units within ten business days, with a miss treated as a Housing Accountability Act violation. That second one is real leverage on close-out timing."
    ]
  },
